1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,090
That guilty look?

2
00:00:01,090 --> 00:00:02,500
It isn't a confession.

3
00:00:02,500 --> 00:00:05,769
In a 2009 study, fourteen
dogs were left with food.

4
00:00:05,769 --> 00:00:10,000
Sometimes owners were told the dog
had eaten, even when it hadn't.

5
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:13,549
When owners returned, the guilty
look tracked the scolding.

6
00:00:13,549 --> 00:00:18,000
Dogs looked guiltier when scolded,
whether or not they had taken the food.

7
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:20,958
In other words, the
look tracked the owner's

8
00:00:20,958 --> 00:00:23,782
cues, not a reliable inner sense of guilt.

9
00:00:23,782 --> 00:00:26,000
The dogs were reading the moment.

10
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:30,575
The paper describes the look as
lowered head, flattened ears,

11
00:00:30,575 --> 00:00:35,000
and a tucked tail — a package
humans readily read as guilt.

12
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:36,818
So skip the interrogation.

13
00:00:36,818 --> 00:00:41,713
Check what actually happened, and
respond to the behavior you can see.

14
00:00:41,713 --> 00:00:45,000
The look is real; the
guilt story is the guess.
